  <section id="s1">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <h2>1. The Neural Co-Processor Problem and the Berkeley Two-Learner Lineage</h2>
      <span class="ref">docs/neural-co-processing.md</span>
    </div>
    <p>A brain–computer interface <strong>decodes</strong> neural activity into control commands; a computer–brain interface <strong>encodes</strong> information back into cortex via stimulation. A <strong>neural co-processor</strong> (Rao 2019, 2020) closes the loop, doing both simultaneously while both the biological brain and the artificial device adapt to each other under a shared objective.</p>
    <p>This problem sits at the intersection of two Berkeley lineages: Jose Carmena's <strong>two-learner BMI program</strong> (Ganguly &amp; Carmena 2009 showing stable cortical neuroprosthetic maps; Orsborn et al. 2014 closed-loop decoder adaptation; Athalye et al. 2017–2020 coordinated neural dynamics; Madduri, Burden &amp; Orsborn 2023 game-theoretic co-adaptation) and Stuart Russell &amp; Anca Dragan's <strong>human-compatible cooperative inference</strong> (assistance games under partial observation). Closed-loop co-processors are already clinical reality — from intracortical tactile feedback (Flesher et al. 2021; Greenspon et al. 2025) and hippocampal CA3→CA1 memory prostheses (Hampson et al. 2018) to Medtronic's FDA-approved BrainSense Adaptive DBS (2025) and inner-speech decoding (Kunz et al., <em>Cell</em> 2025).</p>
    <p>Yet the field lacked an exact mathematical language for two coupled, partially observed learners whose interfaces reshape mid-run. Hyperactivity supplies that language directly — without relying on invasive animal models.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="s2">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <h2>2. Dynamical Descent, Output-Null Fibers, and Intrinsic Manifolds</h2>
      <span class="ref">neuro/descent.cub · lib/observation.cub · lib/fiber.cub</span>
    </div>
    <p>Every neural recording — spike trains, local field potentials, calcium imaging, or fMRI — is a lossy projection \(o : H \to Y\) from high-dimensional biophysical state onto a lower-dimensional readout \(y\). Formalizing this projection through its observation fiber \(\mathsf{Fib}_o(y) = \sum_{h : H} \mathsf{Path}_Y(o(h), y)\) immediately yields three sharp structural theorems:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Non-closure of voltage-only projections (Hodgkin–Huxley obstruction):</strong> Two membrane states with identical voltage \(V\) and identical drive current \(I\), but different sodium-channel inactivation variables \(h_1 \neq h_2\), occupy the same voltage fiber yet yield strictly different voltage derivatives \(\dot{V}_1 \neq \dot{V}_2\). No autonomous differential law descends to voltage alone.</li>
      <li><strong>Intrinsic manifolds as transport cost (Sadtler et al. 2014; Golub et al. 2018):</strong> Motor-cortex population activity resides on a low-dimensional intrinsic manifold. Monkeys rapidly learn new decoders that stay <em>within</em> the manifold, because within-manifold adaptation only requires transporting patterns already in the neural repertoire along a new readout map. Outside-manifold decoders demand states with no preimage in the existing repertoire — and even a decoder change preserving the <em>present</em> output \(D'(x) = D(x)\) rotates the output-null fiber \(\mathsf{Fib}_D(y)\), altering the task gradient and Hessian with respect to neural activity.</li>
      <li><strong>Non-ergodic population averaging:</strong> Averaging trajectories across distinct individuals or heterogeneous cortical ensembles collapses incompatible fibers into a synthetic mean trajectory that need not exist in any single nervous system.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section id="s3">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <h2>3. Two-Learner Co-Adaptation: Symbolic SOS Lyapunov Stability Over \(\mathbb{Q}\)</h2>
      <span class="ref">neuro/lyapunov.cub · chem/algebra.cub</span>
    </div>
    <p>Consider a coupled two-learner system where biological tracking error \(e_B\) and artificial decoder error \(e_C\) evolve with self-adaptation rates \(\eta_B, \eta_C &gt; 0\) and cross-coupling gains \(\gamma_1, \gamma_2\):</p>
    $$\dot{e}_B \;=\; -\eta_B e_B + \gamma_1 e_C, \qquad \dot{e}_C \;=\; \gamma_2 e_B - \eta_C e_C$$
    <p>Choose the weighted quadratic Lyapunov candidate \(V(e_B, e_C) = p_1 e_B^2 + p_2 e_C^2\) with \(p_1, p_2 &gt; 0\). Computing \(-\dot{V} = -2p_1 e_B \dot{e}_B - 2p_2 e_C \dot{e}_C\) yields the quadratic form:</p>
    $$-\dot{V} \;=\; 2p_1\eta_B e_B^2 \;-\; 2(p_1\gamma_1 + p_2\gamma_2)e_B e_C \;+\; 2p_2\eta_C e_C^2$$
    <p>Rather than sampling numerical eigenvalues at isolated parameter values, <code>neuro/lyapunov.cub</code> decides the exact Sum-of-Squares (SOS) completion-of-the-square identity in canonical normal form over the universal polynomial ring over \(\mathbb{Q}\) (<code>al-id</code> in <code>chem/algebra.cub</code>):</p>
    $$2p_1\eta_B\,(-\dot{V}) \;=\; \bigl(2p_1\eta_B e_B - (p_1\gamma_1 + p_2\gamma_2)e_C\bigr)^2 \;+\; \Bigl(4p_1 p_2 \eta_B \eta_C - (p_1\gamma_1 + p_2\gamma_2)^2\Bigr)e_C^2$$
    <div class="thm">
      <b>Universal Stability Certificate — Exact Hurwitz Boundary (neuro/lyapunov.cub)</b>
      Because this identity holds in the universal polynomial ring over \(\mathbb{Q}\), it holds in every commutative ring containing \(\mathbb{Q}\) for <em>all</em> parameter values. Whenever \(4p_1 p_2 \eta_B \eta_C &gt; (p_1\gamma_1 + p_2\gamma_2)^2\), every term on the right is strictly nonnegative, proving global asymptotic stability. Choosing the optimal weight ratio \(p_1 / p_2 = \gamma_2 / \gamma_1\) recovers the exact sharp Hurwitz threshold:
      $$\eta_B\,\eta_C \;>\; \gamma_1\,\gamma_2$$
      Co-adaptation converges if and only if the geometric mean of self-adaptation rates dominates cross-learner interference.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="s4">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <h2>4. Forward-Invariant Safety: Control Barrier Functions &amp; Driven FitzHugh–Nagumo</h2>
      <span class="ref">neuro/barrier.cub · chem/algebra.cub</span>
    </div>
    <p>Stability guarantees eventual convergence, not that a closed-loop neural stimulator stays inside safe biophysical bounds along the way. Safe operation requires <strong>forward invariance</strong> of the safe ball \(h(e_B, e_C) = R^2 - e_B^2 - e_C^2 \ge 0\) via Nagumo's Control Barrier Function (CBF) inequality \(\dot{h} + \alpha h \ge 0\).</p>

    <h3>4.1 Two-Learner CBF Forward Invariance (<code>cbf-2learner-safe-sos-id</code>)</h3>
    <p>Expanding \(\dot{h} + \alpha h = \alpha R^2 + (2\eta_B - \alpha)e_B^2 - 4\gamma e_B e_C + (2\eta_C - \alpha)e_C^2\) and completing the square in the universal polynomial ring over \(\mathbb{Q}\) yields:</p>
    $$(2\eta_B - \alpha)(\dot{h} + \alpha h) \;=\; (2\eta_B - \alpha)\alpha R^2 \;+\; \bigl((2\eta_B - \alpha)e_B - 2\gamma e_C\bigr)^2 \;+\; \Bigl((2\eta_B - \alpha)(2\eta_C - \alpha) - 4\gamma^2\Bigr)e_C^2$$
    <p>Hence forward invariance of the safe ball holds algebraically whenever \(2\eta_B &gt; \alpha &gt; 0\) and \((2\eta_B - \alpha)(2\eta_C - \alpha) \ge 4\gamma^2\).</p>

    <h3>4.2 Externally Driven FitzHugh–Nagumo Excitable Membrane (<code>fhn-driven-trapping-sos-id</code>)</h3>
    <p>For excitable-membrane dynamics under arbitrary external stimulation current \(I\), recovery offset \(a\), and damping \(b &gt; 0\):</p>
    $$\dot{v} \;=\; v - \tfrac{1}{3}v^3 - w + I, \qquad \dot{w} \;=\; \varepsilon(v + a - bw)$$
    <p><code>neuro/barrier.cub</code> proves a universal polynomial SOS trapping-region certificate holding for <em>every</em> \(I, a\) and \(b &gt; 0\) — guaranteeing global boundedness of membrane potential under arbitrary bounded stimulation without numerical integration.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="s5">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <h2>5. Proof-Gated Reshaping Interfaces, Mental Passwords, and Covert Encoding Models</h2>
      <span class="ref">lib/polyproc.cub · neuro/coproc.cub · neuro/reshaping-coproc.cub</span>
    </div>
    <p>Biological and neural interfaces reshape themselves mid-run: ion channels gate open and closed, decoders couple and decouple, and learning installs new sensory channels. In <code>lib/polyproc.cub</code>, guarded processes generalize from static signatures to <strong>indexed interface-reshaping processes</strong> <code>RProc κ D A i</code> over a dynamic signature <code>DynSig</code>, where every interaction \((c, r)\) transitions the active interface state to <code>ds-next D i c r</code> (with an exact cubical round-trip embedding path <code>gproc-rproc-id</code> back to static processes).</p>

    <h3>5.1 Proof-Gated Activation (<code>SafeGateDS</code> &amp; <code>disagreement-blocks-gate</code>)</h3>
    <p><code>neuro/reshaping-coproc.cub</code> constructs a proof-gated neural co-processor with two interface modes: passive probing (<code>false</code>) and active coupled actuation (<code>true</code>). The transition command from <code>false</code> to <code>true</code> carries a <code>SafeAt</code> proof witness over the entire compatible observation fiber as a required field. Unverified actuation is literally <strong>ill-typed</strong>, and any split witness (<code>Disagreement</code>) invokes <code>disagreement-blocks-gate</code>, making activation uninhabited until a discriminating probe collapses the fiber.</p>

    <h3>5.2 Mental-Password Impossibility and Covert Stimulus Optimizers</h3>
    <p>Inner-speech decoders (Kunz et al., <em>Cell</em> 2025) propose gating neural readout behind a "mental password." <code>neuro/coproc.cub</code> proves structurally why a lexical gate cannot constitute consent: surface lexical tokens share fibers with distinct internal intents and involuntary thoughts (<code>lexical-gate-fails</code>). Likewise, whole-brain encoding models that require no implant at all — such as Meta FAIR's TRIBE v2 (2026), which predicts unseen subjects' fMRI responses to video, audio, and text so stimuli can be optimized <em>in silico</em> — are unilateral co-processors with a hidden objective: the platform optimizes stimuli against the brain while leaving the brain's objective fiber completely unconstrained. Hyperactivity's receptive interface inverts this: a transparent bidirectional lens contracts the observer's fiber to a singleton (<code>isContr</code>), sharing the objective and witnessing every step.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="s6">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <h2>6. Cognitive Inhibition, REBUS Prior Relaxation, and Framing Invariance</h2>
      <span class="ref">neuro/inhibition.cub · chem/basin-compose.cub</span>
    </div>
    <p><code>neuro/inhibition.cub</code> formalizes three core phenomena of cognitive control and affective neuroscience over observation fibers and compositional basins:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>REBUS prior relaxation &amp; reduced latent inhibition:</strong> Rigid top-down priors act as aggressive fiber restrictions that prune valid hypotheses before observation can evaluate them. Prior relaxation widens the active fiber, allowing trajectories trapped in rigid local attractors to cross into broader basins.</li>
      <li><strong>Framing-effect invariance (De Martino et al. 2006, 2008):</strong> Two descriptions of the same underlying decision problem related by a value-preserving equivalence must induce identical warranted actions — exposing emotional framing bias as a failure of univalent transport across equivalent presentations.</li>
      <li><strong>Addiction &amp; engagement attractor collapse:</strong> Compulsive engagement loops are modeled as deep, narrow attractor basins that shrink an organism's reachable state space unless broken by structural basin reshaping (<code>MetaFold</code> in <code>chem/basin-compose.cub</code>).</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="s7">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <h2>7. Synthetic Synesthesia and Eye-Contact Co-Processing at the Speed of Light</h2>
      <span class="ref">neuro/synesthesia.cub · neuro/reshaping-coproc.cub · docs/language-is-trained-synesthesia.md</span>
    </div>
    <p>Why drill electrodes into cortex to write low-bandwidth current when the nervous system already possesses gigabit-scale optical and auditory input channels? Spoken and written language are themselves <strong>trained cross-modal synesthesia</strong>: arbitrary acoustic or visual shapes bound through co-occurrence to structured semantic invariants.</p>
    <p><code>neuro/synesthesia.cub</code> formalizes spoken language as a non-sonic concurrent, bidirectional literacy/tickertape transport, memory transport \(M_i(w, t)\), and <strong>population-deviation detector blindness</strong> — proving why any detector calibrated solely on statistical deviation from a population mean becomes blind to a cognitive phenomenon once prevalence approaches 100% (as with universal literacy). In <code>neuro/reshaping-coproc.cub</code>, deliberate training of a new cross-modal binding (<code>SynesthesiaDS</code>, <code>install-synesthesia</code>) is modeled directly as an interface-reshaping transition, while bilateral eye-contact co-processing instantiates <code>coproc-basin-merge</code>: two nervous systems coupling into a shared cognitive light cone at the speed of light, with zero electrodes and zero animal sacrifice.</p>
  </section>

